
## What this dataset cannot answer

The Kids' Recode file is a one-time snapshot, with interviews between November 2017 and April 2018 and no interviews at all from May to October. It can tell me which children were stunted at the time of the survey, but not who became stunted later, how stunting changes over a year, or whether a child flagged today would have improved after a visit. It also contains no information about the Lady Health Workers themselves, so I cannot say which children an LHW already visits, how many households each one covers, or whether a flagged child would actually be reached. The only geography is the region and an anonymous cluster number, so any district-level or catchment-level ranking is outside what this file can support. Finally, the data predate the 2022 floods that hit Sindh badly, so it cannot say how much has changed since.

The biggest risk to the framed question

The biggest risk is the size of the Sindh sample. Of 2,278 Sindh children in the file, 806 were measured, and 27 of those carry the flagged code 9998, which leaves 779 usable scores. The target I framed is recall of at least 0.75 at precision of at least 0.60. If I hold out about a fifth of the Sindh clusters, the test set would hold roughly 150 children, around 75 of them stunted, and by my own rough calculation the 95% interval around a recall of 0.75 on 75 positives would be about plus or minus 0.10. So a model could meet the target on the test set by luck, or miss it by luck, and I would not be able to tell the difference. The precision floor of 0.60 is also not far above the base rate of about 49% (weighted) in Sindh, so the model has to separate children well to clear it.

A second risk is representativeness. Only about a third of children in every province have a score, and the 7,490 "no measurement found in household" cases look similar to the scored children on residence, wealth and mother's education, but I checked only those three variables. If unmeasured households differ in ways I did not check, the model would be trained on a slightly different population from the one the programme serves. A third risk is that rows are not independent: siblings share a mother (4,421 repeated caseid values) and children share a cluster, so a random train/test split would leak information and overstate performance.

Next steps

First, I will build the two baselines on the 779 usable Sindh scores, flag-everyone and the one-line rule (wealth quintile 1-2 and mother with no education), and compute their recall and precision with confidence intervals, before I decide whether the success metric needs to change. Second, I will split by cluster rather than by row, and repeat the split several times (grouped cross-validation) so I can report how much recall and precision move from one split to the next, not a single number. Third, I will train on all provinces with the survey weights, as in the framing formula, and compare it with a Sindh-only model to see whether the extra provinces help or hurt. Fourth, I will report performance separately for rural and urban children and by wealth group, because the programme serves mostly rural and slum communities. Fifth, I will read the PDHS survey report to find out how the households for height and weight measurement were selected, so that I can say with evidence whether the missing children are missing at random. Last, before any real use, the model would need to be re-validated on newer data, because of the 2022 floods and because the survey covers only part of the year.
